{"kind":"insufficient_data","text":"Microsoft’s FY{{F2_fiscal_year}} fiscal year ended {{F2_end}}. Revenue was {{F2_value}} {{F2_unit}}, while cost of revenue was {{F3_value}} {{F3_unit}} and operating expenses were {{F4_value}} {{F4_unit}}. Compared with the prior fiscal year, revenue increased, but cost of revenue grew faster; operating expenses also increased, at a slower pace than revenue. The company attributed revenue growth mainly to Microsoft Cloud, including Azure and Microsoft 365 Commercial cloud, and said higher cost of revenue reflected Microsoft Cloud growth. It also reported that gross margin percentage declined slightly, citing continued AI-infrastructure investment and growing AI-product usage, partly offset by Microsoft Cloud efficiency gains. [Source 9] [Source 12]

